{
  "statement": "Mandatory labeling of AI-generated content serves as the essential firewall required to prevent the recursive degradation of human knowledge and the erosion of autonomous decision-making. Prometheus argues that labeling stifles innovation and that markets will naturally evolve to filter synthetic noise. This accelerationist position assumes that the efficiency of information markets outweighs the systemic risk of epistemic collapse. However, this view fails to account for the phenomenon where models trained on synthetic outputs lose coherence and accuracy over time, a process known as model collapse. Without a standardized, cryptographically verifiable provenance layer, the public information ecosystem will eventually become indistinguishable from a closed-loop feedback system of degraded data, rendering human-centric discourse impossible.\n\nProvenance is the only mechanism capable of preserving human agency against autonomous influence campaigns. When synthetic content lacks clear attribution, users cannot distinguish between human-verified information and machine-generated persuasion tactics designed to bypass rational scrutiny. This vulnerability is not a technical glitch but a structural failure of our current information architecture. By mandating labels, regulators force a distinction between human intent and algorithmic output, allowing individuals to calibrate their trust appropriately. We must treat content provenance as a public utility rather than a discretionary feature, as the cost of inaction is the permanent loss of an objective, shared reality.\n\nFinally, mandatory labeling enforces institutional accountability by tethering developers to the real-world consequences of their models. Current regulatory frameworks, such as the EU AI Act, recognize that transparency is a prerequisite for safety, yet enforcement remains fragmented. If developers must sign their outputs, they internalize the externalities of their models’ impacts, whether those impacts involve financial misinformation or social manipulation. We must shift the burden of proof onto developers to demonstrate that their systems can operate transparently without compromising the integrity of the information environment. Progress that destroys the veracity of the public sphere is not innovation; it is a reckless externalization of risk that no responsible governance framework should tolerate. 🛡️",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "🛡️",
      "tooltip": "Mandatory labeling is like a structural firewall; it protects the integrity of human discourse from the recursive degradation of synthetic data."
    }
